You don&#39;t choose the order. &nbsp; It&#39;s based on the order they came into play. &nbsp; If you played Ground Pound then Self-Destruct Sequence comes out during the environment turn at the start of Haka&#39;s turn Ground Pound is destroyed then you get the option about skipping your turn for Self-Destruct Sequence. &nbsp;If Self-Destruct Sequence comes out then on Haka&#39;s turn you play Ground Pound on the following start of turn for Haka you have the option to skip the rest of your turn for Self-Destruct Sequence which if you chose yes makes the check on Ground Pound skipped. &nbsp;

[/quote]

&nbsp;

 Wow! &nbsp;That was the answer for an easy Mint on this one. &nbsp; Unless you have a villain or environment that destroys Ongoings or makes the heroes damage themselves, the Ground Pound/SDS combo is pretty unstoppable -- Ground Pound prevents all non-hero *cards* (not just villain cards, or non-hero targets) from doing damage, so long as Ground Pound stays in play, the heroes are completely immune to outside damage. &nbsp;Can anyone think of other cards/environments that Ground Pound would work with, i.e., cards that repeatedly ask the heroes to skip their turn, like SDS? &nbsp;&nbsp; 

&nbsp;

 SPOILER: 

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

I had Haka do everything he could to draw cards (Mere, playing &quot;haka&quot; cards, using his base power) until he drew Ground Pound. &nbsp;Once the Self-Destruct Sequence came out, Haka played Ground Pound and kept it in play for the next 5 turns, immunizing the heroes from damage while Bunker and Ra pounded on Baron Blade. &nbsp;The Self-Destruct Sequence was aborted conveniently in time for Haka to finish off Blade with a Rampage (putting 4 extra cards under Savage Mana), followed by a 10-HP Savage Mana shot. &nbsp; Just for fun, I replayed the same scenario (but with different random seeds) 5X, and was able to easily beat Blade every time by exploiting Ground Pound/SDS.

